Induction Dirge

Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + BEBOP + MELANCHOLIC, NOSTALGIC & SAD + Tuba  
Creative Catalyst: It began as protest music performed on hacked traffic infrastructure.

Induction Dirge subverts the lounge-sleek polish of nu jazz and the hyperactive virtuosity of bebop by routing blistering brass runs through the rusted guts of municipal electronics. Instead of soaring trumpets, a heavily amplified tuba commands the front of the stage, executing heart-wrenching chromatic acrobatics that plummet into resonant sub-bass drops. Backed by time-stretched pedestrian walk-signal chirps, degraded Rhodes chords, and polyrhythmic breakbeats sampled from relays and traffic-flow induction sensors, the genre turns city-scale commuter frustration into deep, tear-stained civic lamentation.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: Fluctuating between 85 and 110 BPM with sudden, disorienting rubato decelerations that mirror gridlocked traffic; bebop's syncopated ride-cymbal swing is reconstructed from the mechanical click-clack of street-crossing switches and fractured breakbeats.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark bebop contrafacts and altered dominant scales (Charlie Parker reharmonized through a funeral lens) built over descending minor 9ths, weeping flat fifths, and suspended chords that refuse to resolve.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Inductive Sensor Sidechaining: Sub-bass and tuba envelopes are sidechained directly to the voltage swings of hacked copper induction road loops, ducking dynamically as ghost traffic passes.
  2. Crosswalk Granularization: Monotone pedestrian crossing beeps and acoustic accessibility cues are pitched down two octaves and stretched into smeared, melancholic synth pads.
  3. Pneumatic Transit Convolution Reverb (Impossible Technique): The tuba bell is sealed into decommissioned subterranean pneumatic mail tubes, capturing impossible physical impulse responses that stretch across three miles of underground brass piping, shifting pitch whenever city transit doors open or close.
- Dynamics: Starts in near-inaudible isolation—just the spit-valve hiss of the tuba and low hum of asphalt—before cascading into dizzying, high-speed valve flurries that swell into deafening, asphalt-shaking walls of brass and digital distortion, then suddenly collapse back into naked, sorrowful tape decay.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre ignited during the three-week municipal transit blackout of autumn 2031 in the canal-side tunnels of Rotterdam, where transit-worker strikes collided with evicted conservatory dropouts. Calling themselves "Switch-Jockeys," rogue brass players and hardware modders cracked open the yellow metal control pillars at major intersections, tapping the 24-volt lines to power micro-samplers and feeding inductive road sensors directly into modular gear. Dressed in oil-stained high-visibility vests tailored into funeral drapes and wearing contact mics clipped to wet wool lapels, they occupied dead intersections at 3 AM. Their ritual was sonic civil disobedience: locking traffic lights on blinking amber, turning pedestrian signals into stuttering metronomes, and unleashing mournful, virtuosic tuba solos that reverberated down miles of stalled, silent ring roads.

Central Instruments: Modified CC Tuba (fitted with leadpipe piezo pickups and electronic mute baffles), hacked induction-loop decoders (transmuting road capacitance into MIDI data), Fender Rhodes Mark II through tape echo, MPC-style granular drum samplers, Moog Matriarch for auxiliary bass drones, and re-tuned pedestrian walk-signal boxes.

For Fans Of: Theon Cross, Colin Stetson, Bohren & der Club of Gore, The Comet Is Coming

